<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dunkirk</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dunkerque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dunkirk is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> in the Nord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, close to the Belgian border. Part of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it changed hands repeatedly between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> bought it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II of England</a> in 1662. In 1940, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, more than 330,000 Allied soldiers were evacuated from its harbour and beaches.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunkirk.jpg" alt="Dunkirk" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Velvet · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunkerque_hotel_de_ville_vu_port.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
